        "raw_text": "\n--- Page 186 ---\n102 b.]\nCALOULATION OF CAPACITY.\n151\nto the surfaces for which $\\Psi_{1}$ is constant, and if we denote the\nlength of this line measured from $8_{0}$ by 8 , then\n$$R \\frac{d x}{d s}=-\\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d x}, \\quad R \\frac{d y}{d s}=-\\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d y}, \\quad R \\frac{d z}{d s}=-\\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d z},$$\n(12)\nwhere $R$ is the resultant intensity $=-\\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d s}$, so that\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d p}{d x} \\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d x}+\\frac{d p}{d y} \\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d y}+\\frac{d p}{d z} \\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d z} & =-R \\frac{d p}{d s} \\\\\n& =R^{2} \\frac{d p}{d \\Psi_{1}}\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(13)\nand equation (11) becomes\nwhence\n$$\\begin{gathered}\np \\nabla^{2} \\Psi=R^{2} \\frac{d p}{d \\Psi_{1}} \\\\\np=C \\exp \\cdot \\int_{0}^{\\Psi_{1}} \\frac{\\nabla^{2} \\Psi_{1}}{R^{2}} d \\Psi_{1},\n\\end{gathered}$$\n(14)\n(15)\nthe integral being a line integral taken along the line $s$.\nLet us next assume that along the line 8 ,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&-\\frac{d \\Psi_{2}}{d s}=f \\frac{d x}{d s}+g \\frac{d y}{d s}+h \\frac{d z}{d s} \\\\\n&=-p \\frac{d \\Psi_{1}}{d s} \\\\\n& \\Psi_{2}=C \\int_{0}^{*}\\left(\\exp \\cdot \\int \\frac{\\nabla^{2} \\Psi_{1}}{R^{2}} d \\Psi_{1}\\right) d \\Psi_{1},\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(16)\n(17)\nthen\nthe integration being always understood to be performed along\nthe line $s$.\nThe constant $C$ is now to be determined from the condition\nthat $\\Psi_{2}=1$ at $s_{1}$ when also $\\Psi_{1}=1$, so that\n$$C \\int_{0}^{1}\\left\\{\\exp \\cdot \\int_{0}^{\\Psi} \\frac{\\nabla^{2} \\Psi}{R^{2}} d \\Psi\\right\\} d \\Psi=1$$\n(18)\nThis gives a second approximation to $\\Psi$, and the process may\nbe repeated.\nThe results obtained from calculating $W_{\\Psi_{1}}, W_{\\mathcal{D}_{2}}, W_{\\Psi_{2}}$, \\&c., give\ncapacities alternately above and below the true capacity and\ncontinually approximating thereto.\nThe process as indicated above involves the calculation of the\nform of the line 8 and integration along this line, operations\nwhich are in general too difficult for practical purposes.\n",
